 "lq": [
  {
   "q": "1. Find the Z-transform of \\(x(n) = 3^n \\cos\\left(\\frac{n\\pi}{4}\\right) u(n) + 2 \\delta(n-3)\\) . 2. Using properties, find the Z-transform of \\(x(n) = n 2^{n-1} u(n-1)\\) . 3. If \\(Z\\{x(n)\\} = \\frac{z}{(z-2)^2}\\) , find \\(Z\\{n x(n)\\}\\) and \\(Z\\{2^n x(n)\\}\\) . 4. Determine the sequence \\(x(n)\\) whose Z-transform is given by \\(X(z) = \\log\\left(\\frac{z}{z-1}\\right)\\) , for \\(|z|>1\\) . (Hint: Use series expansion of log(1+x))",
   "answer": [
    "1. \\(x(n) = 3^n \\cos(n\\pi/4) u(n) + 2 \\delta(n-3)\\) : Using linearity. \\(Z\\{3^n \\cos(n\\pi/4) u(n)\\} = \\frac{z(z-3\\cos(\\pi/4))}{z^2 - 6z\\cos(\\pi/4) + 9} = \\frac{z(z-3/\\sqrt{2})}{z^2 - (6/\\sqrt{2})z + 9}\\) . \\(Z\\{2\\delta(n-3)\\} = 2z^{-3}\\) . So, \\(X(z) = \\frac{z(z-3/\\sqrt{2})}{z^2 - (3\\sqrt{2})z + 9} + \\frac{2}{z^3}\\) .",
    "2. \\(x(n) = n 2^{n-1} u(n-1)\\) : We know \\(Z\\{n a^n u(n)\\} = \\frac{az}{(z-a)^2}\\) . For a=2, \\(Z\\{n 2^n u(n)\\} = \\frac{2z}{(z-2)^2}\\) . Now, \\(n 2^{n-1} u(n-1) = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (n-1+1) 2^{n-1} u(n-1) = \\frac{1}{2}[ (n-1)2^{n-1} u(n-1) + 2^{n-1} u(n-1) ]\\) . Using shifting: \\(Z\\{(n-1)2^{n-1} u(n-1)\\} = z^{-1} \\cdot \\frac{2z}{(z-2)^2} = \\frac{2}{z(z-2)^2}\\) . And \\(Z\\{2^{n-1} u(n-1)\\} = z^{-1} \\cdot \\frac{z}{z-2} = \\frac{1}{z-2}\\) . So, \\(X(z) = \\frac{1}{2} \\left[ \\frac{2}{z(z-2)^2} + \\frac{1}{z-2} \\right] = \\frac{1}{z(z-2)^2} + \\frac{1}{2(z-2)}\\) .",
    "3. Given \\(X(z)=\\frac{z}{(z-2)^2}\\) : i) \\(Z\\{n x(n)\\} = -z \\frac{d}{dz} X(z) = -z \\cdot \\frac{(z-2)^2 - 2z(z-2)}{(z-2)^4} = -z \\cdot \\frac{-(z+2)(z-2)}{(z-2)^4} = \\frac{z(z+2)}{(z-2)^3}\\) . ii) \\(Z\\{2^n x(n)\\} = X\\left(\\frac{z}{2}\\right) = \\frac{(z/2)}{((z/2)-2)^2} = \\frac{z/2}{( (z-4)/2 )^2} = \\frac{z/2}{(z-4)^2 / 4} = \\frac{2z}{(z-4)^2}\\) .",
    "4. \\(X(z) = \\log\\left(\\frac{z}{z-1}\\right) = -\\log\\left(1-\\frac{1}{z}\\right)\\) for |z|>1. Use expansion: \\(\\log(1-y) = -\\sum_{m=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{y^m}{m}\\) for |y|<1. Here, \\(y=1/z\\) , so \\(X(z) = \\sum_{m=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{(1/z)^m}{m} = \\sum_{m=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{m} z^{-m}\\) . Comparing with \\(X(z)=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} x(n) z^{-n}\\) , we get: \\(x(0)=0\\) , and for \\(n \\ge 1\\) , \\(x(n) = \\frac{1}{n}\\) . So, \\(x(n) = \\begin{cases} 0, & n=0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{n}, & n \\ge 1 \\end{cases}\\) ."
   ]
  }
